1. Mobile App Defects

App Chosen: Paytm – Login/Signup Form

Defect 1. OTP is not received even after clicking the “Send OTP” button.
Defect 2. The login button remains enabled when the mobile number field is empty.
Defect 3. An invalid 5-digit mobile number is accepted without showing an error message.
Defect 4. After entering a valid OTP, the app keeps loading and does not redirect the user to the home screen.
Defect 5. The “Send OTP” button text overlaps with the button boundary on smaller mobile screens.


 2. Severity and Priority

 Defect                                                       Severity     Priority   
                                                                             
 OTP is not received after clicking “Send OTP”                Critical     High      
 Login button enabled with an empty mobile number             Major        Medium 
 Invalid 5-digit mobile number accepted                       Major        High   
 Valid OTP entered but app does not redirect to home screen   High         High   
 “Send OTP” button text overlaps on small screens             Minor        Low    

Justification where Severity and Priority are different

Defect: Login button is enabled when the mobile number field is empty.

Severity: Major because it affects the login validation and can confuse users.
Priority: Medium because users may still be able to enter a valid mobile number and log in successfully. Therefore, it is important to fix, but it is less urgent than defects that completely block login.



4. Scenario: UPI Payment Defect
            
   After a UPI payment feature was released, users reported that payments were sometimes deducted from their bank account but the app showed the transaction as failed.
   Since the defect was found after release, the company had to investigate user complaints, fix the issue, and handle payment reconciliation.
   This caused financial confusion, customer complaints, and loss of trust in the app.
   If the defect had been found during development, developers could have fixed and tested it before release at a much lower cost.
   Early detection would have prevented user impact and reduced the time and resources needed for the fix.
